Z||000002||hugh||08-20-2000||12:38 PM||||My engine cover is very heavy,I used the front trunk support and it worked perfectly in my application.I can't open my engine cover as high as most because of the scoop.Just relocating the mounts should make this kind of support acceptable for most covers.If you want to see it go to--http://users.snip.net/~hlemma/fierotropen.jpg <A HREF="http://users.snip.net/~hlemma/fierotropen2.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://users.snip.net/~hlemma/fierotropen2.jpg</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||08-20-2000||12:49 PM||||I've often wondered if the way to go wasn't to look for a late model GM car at the boneyard that uses the gas struts on the trunk/hood and use one of these (or both) on the decklid.<P>Taking them off a wreck would allow you to get all the hardware.<P>I may try this next time I'm at the boneyard...maybe someone who's going there before me can consider this and get back to the board.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||08-20-2000||06:12 PM||||some one asked about this a few weeks back. i dont think there is a part # since it's not a stock part. i would guess the owner got them from rodney dickman. saw it at the fiero show. i gave a # for one i got at auto zone, but mine is very strong and i try not to let my trunk raise without my holding it. the up side though is that it cost me about 20 bucks i think (cost of strut, a couple of inches piece of 1 1/2" angle iron and some hardware). i have a fairly heavy mustang wing on my '87 and it still throws it right up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||08-20-2000||10:05 PM||||They have part numbers and/or a car they came off of. <BR>Philphine, that's what I'm afraid of!<P>Someone has 'em fork over the part #'s<BR>||||reg||1
